    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sea-surface raw fluorescence measurements were used to estimate surface chlorophyll-a concentrations along a northern tropical Pacific Ocean transect. Estimates of surface chlorophyll showed significant correlations with both thermocline and chlorophyll maximum layer depths, increasing surface concentrations indicating a shallower thermocline and chlorophyll maximum layer. An increase in surface chlorophyll near the equator coincided with the boundary between the North Equatorial Current and the Equatorial Countercurrent, indicating a disruption of the thermocline by the turbulent boundary layer of the two opposing currents. Thus surface chlorophyll concentrations were found to indicate both physical and biological features of the water column.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
